WEBVTT

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:03.580
The first beer drunk by a Japanese person

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.880
is said to have been a Dutch beer
presented to the Tokugawa Shogunate.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:33.050
Christmas.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.470
People's white breath as they pass us by.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:40.090
The warm city lights cast
an even greater shine upon it

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.260
projecting a myriad of colours
up to the heavens.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:49.310
From the beginning of December,
bars are already all Christmassy.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.860
They welcome regular customers.

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:57.700
Customers working a little.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.280
Customers one rarely sees in bars.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:04.910
And eager couples.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:09.960
Of course, we bartenders hope that
each of our patrons

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:13.210
will be able to enjoy
a nice Christmas moment...

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:14.710
But then...

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.260
One of our biggest difficulties is

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:26.180
when a customer who seems not to fit
the ambiance enters on their own.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.730
But it doesn't change
how he greets them.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:38.780
Welcome.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.580
Suddenly, the customer's nerves completely
melt away, as though under a spell.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.870
And before he realises it,
he's sitting at the bar.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:53.920
But he doesn't exactly fit
the atmosphere, now does he?

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:57.920
Are you on your own, sir?
Or are you waiting for someone?

00:02:58.550 --> 00:02:59.760
No...

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:03.220
A star... I found a star.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.930
A star, you say?

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.730
Yes. I was following it,
then I saw it come in here.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.230
Not the ideal situation, really.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:13.850
Quite.

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:18.230
These kinds of customers usually
have some issues, shall we say.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.780
If there were a couple,
he'd pick a fight with the boyfriend.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.320
If it were a woman on her own,
he'd try to pick her up.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:27.990
Staring intently at the person,
really creepily.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.750
Completely destroying the bar's ambiance.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:36.000
You have to kick people
like this out, am I right?

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:39.130
I guess we'll wait and see first...

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.130
Wait and see, eh.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.930
Well I certainly saw it.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.640
I saw his soul get sucked out by a star

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:52.060
and get pulled straight into that bar.

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.900
But this fella's never
even set foot in a bar.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.320
Nope. He looks Iike the kinda guy
who hangs around food stalls.

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:05.240
But you could see that
he was dead-set on this one.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:08.830
Yep. The last time he'd been that
serious about anything was way back...

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:16.540
When he was a young lad,

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.960
he was just an everyday boy,
one you might see in any old town.

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.800
But one summer's day,

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.050
he tried opening the tap
to take a drink and quench his thirst.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:29.970
And in that moment...

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.150
The boy was entranced by
the water's sparkle.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.780
He wondered why water spilling out

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.160
from the tap looked so beautiful.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:51.120
To him it was as though that sparkle was
coming from stars which only he could see.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:58.290
The boy wanted to make
that sparkle all his own.

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:00.170
That was all.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.340
But that was where his luck ran out.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:08.340
To solve the mystery of
the sparkling flow of water,

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:12.970
he got into the incomprehensible field
known as "computational fluid dynamics".

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.520
He lost all interest in catching insects
and making snowmen,

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:20.440
and devoted himself entirely to
his studies, barely ever leaving his desk.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.900
He eventually became successful enough
to stand at a university podium.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.950
But now, when he turns on a tap,

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:31.700
he can't see the sparkle
in the water anymore.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:36.960
Unsurprisingly, even after he hit his 40s,
there was no hint of romance in his life.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:40.960
To top it off, even if he drank,
he couldn't taste the flavour.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.590
Water and spirits
were all the same to him,

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.260
just topics for debate
in the field of fluid dynamics...

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.510
He was no longer able to see the stars
he once saw in flowing water.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.600
And tonight he has
an elementary school reunion.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:59.480
But, for some reason,
he has a little spring in his step...

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.610
And justifiably so, after all,
the reunion is being held in Ginza.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.030
Having decided he would show off
the results of his studies to everyone,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.240
he had lovingly packed with him
the thesis that earned him his doctorate.

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.660
The location was a private room
in a fancy restaurant.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.960
He was quite alright until
he slid the door open.

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:25.460
But no one was interested
in fluid dynamics.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:27.970
They were all too busy having fun...

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:31.470
You might even say that whatever they
spoke about went straight over his head.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.560
This is what happens when
you dedicate your life to studying.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:40.140
Now that he was here, he realised just
how boring a man he had become.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.730
In this state of mind,
the sparkly, shining Ginza lights

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:48.650
to him felt like no more
than blinding eyesores.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:53.780
He was reminded of a Christmas story
he was told in childhood.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.240
'Twas Christmas night,

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:02.460
In a warm room, there were four candles lit.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:07.590
These candles were there
to watch over the world.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.720
But, on this night,

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:14.300
the candles spoke out sadly.

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:17.810
My name is Peace.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:20.940
But people fight continuously.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.980
It seems that I am not needed.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:25.360
So she said,

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.530
as her flame went out,
flickering fiercely.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:33.820
My name is Faith.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:37.580
But the world tries not to know God.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:40.790
Then may they remain ignorant.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.330
And she too, sadly lamented,
as her flame went out.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:50.920
My name is Love.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:55.010
I had long believed that
somebody would find me,

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.430
but they are all
too engrossed in themselves.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.020
Confronted with people who have lost
sight of the ones that they love,

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:03.350
I sadly depart.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:07.770
Then, at last, a single candle remained.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.530
But the name of the last
candle that stood was...

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:14.780
Hope.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.200
It's a star!

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:24.960
I see a star!

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:35.630
Now, after countless years,
he finally saw another star.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.720
And he remembered...

00:08:37.930 --> 00:08:41.930
the bright, sparkling stars he saw
at that fountain, when he was a boy.

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:43.140
But...

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:47.520
stars don't let themselves
get caught so easily.

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.120
Everyone's had that feeling before...

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.460
The feeling you get
when you first set foot in a bar.

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:13.550
Right. The threshold feels strangely high
and it's somehow difficult to go in.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:17.300
But a strong will can move even mountains.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:24.850
The door opened,
almost as though it sensed his hesitance.

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:28.940
That was his first strange encounter
with bar Eden hall.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:38.490
There remained one candle.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:43.620
The child ran up to the three candles
that had been extinguished and said...

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:45.750
"You mustn't go out!"

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:50.630
"Peace, Faith and Love can't go out!"

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:53.000
"You must all stay alight."

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:59.050
As the child spoke, and tears welled up,
the remaining candle said...

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:02.970
"It is alright, child.
Because my name...

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:04.720
is Hope."

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:10.440
"As long as my flame burns,
we will all keep shining."

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:14.110
Are you sure...?

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:17.900
You don't think I'm somewhat
out of place at this bar?

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:20.360
Absolutely not.
That could never be the case.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.200
As long as you're drinking at this bar,

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:25.240
you are our customer.

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:26.870
Well said!

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.460
I am Kyoji Okita, a professor of
computational fluid dynamics.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:32.670
You may call me "Professor".

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:34.000
Yes, Professor.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:36.460
Ah yes, here's my business card.

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:37.880
SAUCY GIRLS JUST WAITING FOR YOU
"CALL ME"

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.640
Damn it, that's the wrong one!

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:45.060
Professor, please don't worry
about the business card.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.020
PIease just quieten down a bit.

00:10:47.270 --> 00:10:48.890
Anyway, what would you Iike to drink?

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:51.060
What would I Iike...?

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.570
Well, yes... Erm...

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:58.570
Actually, didn't you say
you found a star in here?

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:01.990
What about a star for Christmas?

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:03.570
Romantic, no?

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.200
Champagne?

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:06.740
Have you heard?

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:10.500
A single bottle of champagne has
200 million bubbles locked inside it.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:12.500
It's like the night sky.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:15.170
Which is why, in the Champagne region,

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:18.170
they call drinking champagne,
"drinking stars".

00:11:19.670 --> 00:11:21.430
Can I interest you?

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:25.890
What poppycock. Champagne is just
an extravagance for the rich!

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:28.270
Anyone that gets close
to that stuff becomes corrupt.

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:32.900
We academics live only for the truth,
no matter how poor it might make us.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:37.440
Academia is a garden where all are judged
by the absolute quality of "truth"

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.110
permitted only to
the aristocracy of the mind.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:41.860
Right?

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:43.780
How about something else then?

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:47.700
An academic drinks beer.
A dark one, today, I think.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:51.830
But why do academics drink beer?

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:55.250
The American physicist, who won the Nobel
prize in elementary particle research,

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:59.300
Donald Glaser, came up with
his theory after seeing the beer bubbles.

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:02.550
Had he been all giddy from
drinking luxury champagne,

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:04.760
he probably wouldn't have
made the discovery.

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:08.310
So if I am to follow in
his footsteps and win a Nobel prize,

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:09.560
I'll stick to beer.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:14.190
In that case, Professor,
here is your dark beer.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:27.570
Professor?

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:32.200
It's exquisite. Beer truly is exquisite...

00:12:32.540 --> 00:12:33.540
But...

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:38.880
But despite that, tonight
my heart was stolen by a shining star.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:43.420
Yet even if that star
were to bestow a smile upon me,

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:46.050
I could only endure it in solitude.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:49.050
I know... I just know.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:51.970
Yes. It is only a pipe dream.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:56.480
The world of champagne where the stars
shine and my world of beer...

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:58.610
the two will never meet!

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:02.780
And that's okay I guess...

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:07.780
How about trying beer and
champagne together in that case?

00:13:08.030 --> 00:13:10.910
Huh? What?

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:12.120
What d'you just say?

00:13:12.490 --> 00:13:15.410
I said, we could mix the two together.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:18.080
What? You're nuts, buddy!

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:22.000
Fools really are the happiest folk.

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:23.090
Look here.

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:25.840
This is a fundamental equation
in fluid dynamics.

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:27.430
The Navier-Stokes equation.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:31.600
It is a differential equation used
to calculate the liquid movement

00:13:31.720 --> 00:13:34.350
of a falling or flowing viscous liquid.

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:38.940
Or, put otherwise, it is difficult to mix
two liquids of different density,

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:42.900
velocity, pressure and viscosity.

00:13:43.940 --> 00:13:45.690
Is what I'm getting at.

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:48.030
Is that so?

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:52.700
And anyway, I hardly think such opposing
flavours could ever complement each other.

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:55.250
Why don't you try it, at least?

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:59.000
If it's a failure, tonight's
drinks will be on the house.

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:00.670
What's that?

00:14:01.750 --> 00:14:05.510
Even you... Even you want
to make a mockery of my life?

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:09.260
Do you know how much time I've
spent trying to work out this equation?

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:12.010
How much of my life I've spent on it...

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:15.180
If you deny this equation,
you may as well deny my life's work!

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:20.770
Very well! If I lose, I will gift you this
thesis, which I hold very dearly.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:23.610
Err... It's okay, really...

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:26.440
Now then. If what you say is true,

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.990
prove it to me, on top of this thesis!

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:33.120
I don't think we need go that far.

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:35.990
No, if you are confident,
you should be able to do it.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.080
Now, go ahead...

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:41.500
Now. Now. Now!

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:46.630
Well then.

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:48.300
Let's mix the two.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:08.570
What a waste!
It's such an expensive drink too...

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:12.110
It really was a waste of such
expensive champagne.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:13.570
That's what everyone thought.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:17.160
But why would Sasakura try such
an outrageous move...?

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:22.080
Because he is
the Glass of the Gods, of course.

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:26.460
Young bartender,
I have already beaten you.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:30.510
Simply pouring from left and right from
two different weight bottles

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:34.090
with differing neck diameters, at the same
time, would be a godly feat in itself.

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:39.220
Not forgetting what happens when you
pour two carbonated drinks into a glass.

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:41.390
The whole thing is sure to froth over.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:44.520
Spilling alcohol everywhere is
a bartender's cardinal sin.

00:15:45.020 --> 00:15:47.360
You might as well apologise in advance.

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:15.930
Please enjoy your Black Velvet.

00:16:19.510 --> 00:16:20.220
No way.

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:22.640
The professor probably thought that, too.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:27.650
I mean, it's the first time even I've seen
a Black Velvet poured with two hands.

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:31.650
What's more, I'd say there can't be more
than one in five bartenders who can pour

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.240
that cocktail as fast as he did.

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:36.490
One more reason why they call him

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.450
the Glass of the Gods.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:40.120
It's a miracle.

00:16:40.370 --> 00:16:42.370
It's not spilled over at all...

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:46.040
With a little control over the flow
and the foam rate,

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:47.290
anybody can do it.

00:16:49.170 --> 00:16:52.800
Black Velvet.
Like the black velvety fabric, eh.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:57.720
Velvet comes from the Latin meaning
the soft fuzz that grows on deer antlers.

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:01.140
What a pompous name.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:05.890
But what about the taste...?
Have the two flavours really melded?

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:08.310
This is the real test.

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:11.150
Please, enjoy.

00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:22.660
What on earth is this?

00:17:23.330 --> 00:17:25.750
A light bitter taste,
with a refined sourness.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:29.630
A beer that goes beyond beer...
A champagne that transcends itself!

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:34.920
Like golden velvet it is both
delicate and incredibly smooth.

00:17:35.050 --> 00:17:38.180
If I had to draw comparisons...
It is almost...

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:39.720
AImost...

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:41.850
AImost...

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:45.520
Like Black Velvet!

00:17:49.020 --> 00:17:52.270
Tell me... How is such a thing possible?

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:55.490
Well, I don't know the intricate details.

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:59.950
But alcohol is not merely a liquid.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:03.200
It isn't mere liquid?

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:06.960
That's right. Alcohol contains a spirit.

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:08.330
A soul...

00:18:08.710 --> 00:18:11.960
Which is why it has the ability
to raise people's spirits.

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:13.920
The spirit...

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:17.260
can surpass even science, you mean?

00:18:17.510 --> 00:18:20.720
And even give rise to miracles.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:25.350
I lose.

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:29.140
This is the thesis that
got me my doctorate.

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:31.100
Please, take it.

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:33.770
Thank you but I couldn't.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:35.530
Because...

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:39.110
flaunting one's abilities
in front of customers

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.200
is unbecoming of a bartender.

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:45.290
"Unbecoming of a bartender."

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:47.450
Not quite...

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:50.540
He was not flaunting,

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:55.420
but wanted the professor to enjoy himself
somehow, so he wouldn't stick out.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:00.880
Ryu Sasakura, the Glass of the Gods,
is that kind of bartender.

00:19:01.430 --> 00:19:02.390
Oh, right!

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:06.890
That star the professor was looking for...
who was that?

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:09.520
It wasn't me.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:11.390
-Not me either.
-Not me either.

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:14.730
Well, in that case...

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:21.400
Actually, it was little old me!

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:25.450
The truth is, Mr Okita looked
like Santa Claus to me.

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:28.660
He says such weird and
funny things all the time.

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:31.460
The atmosphere in the bar
became lighter, right?

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:33.920
Which is why...

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:39.170
A star!

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:41.970
Hey, Santa. Thank you.

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:49.930
It's...

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:52.310
It's a miracle!

00:19:53.060 --> 00:19:53.940
Indeed.

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:57.650
At the bar, lots of miracles take place...

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:00.740
And so,

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:04.360
miracles turn into
hope and continue to sparkle

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:08.450
as they climb towards the snowy night sky.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:12.410
As they call out
to all the people and all the glasses,

00:20:12.620 --> 00:20:16.250
a very Merry Christmas.

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:00.800
Merry Christmas, Professor.

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:14.310
RECIPE: CHAMPAGNE - 1/2 GUINNESS - 1/2

00:22:26.920 --> 00:22:30.760
Project supervisor: Justine Cocquyt
Spotting: Charlotte Colart

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:38.890
